Elite QA Team Criteria for Food Delivery Platform (Uber Eats/Hungerstation Type): Complete Bug Detection & Prevention System
Your food delivery platform operates in a complex multi-stakeholder environment with customers, restaurants, drivers, and admins. A top-tier QA team focuses on critical vulnerabilities unique to this business model that can cause revenue loss, reputation damage, and service disruptions.

Critical Security & Payment Vulnerabilities
Payment Processing & Financial Security​

Elite teams examine payment flows with extreme scrutiny since food delivery apps handle thousands of daily transactions. Key focus areas include:

Payment gateway integration testing: Verify PCI DSS compliance, encryption of card data in transit and at rest, proper handling of refunds and chargebacks, failed transaction recovery mechanisms

Double payment prevention: Test race conditions where users might be charged twice for the same order during network interruptions or rapid button clicks

Refund abuse detection: Check for vulnerabilities where users can claim non-delivery and get refunds while keeping the food (friendly fraud)​

Coupon/promo code exploitation: Test for race conditions allowing multiple redemptions of single-use codes, expired coupon validation bypass, stacking restrictions, percentage calculation errors​

Commission calculation accuracy: Verify restaurant commission calculations are correct across different pricing tiers, ensure percentage-based fees calculate properly with taxes and discounts, check for rounding errors in split payments​

Critical Test Cases:

php
// VULNERABLE - Coupon can be redeemed multiple times via race condition
public function applyCoupon(Request $request) {
    $coupon = Coupon::where('code', $request->code)->first();
    if ($coupon->usage_count < $coupon->max_uses) {
        $coupon->increment('usage_count'); // Race condition here
        return $this->applyDiscount($coupon);
    }
}

// SECURE - Use database locking
public function applyCoupon(Request $request) {
    DB::transaction(function() use ($request) {
        $coupon = Coupon::where('code', $request->code)
            ->lockForUpdate()
            ->first();
        
        if (!$coupon || $coupon->usage_count >= $coupon->max_uses) {
            throw new CouponInvalidException();
        }
        
        if ($coupon->expires_at && $coupon->expires_at < now()) {
            throw new CouponExpiredException();
        }
        
        $coupon->increment('usage_count');
        return $this->applyDiscount($coupon);
    });
}
Real-Time Order & Inventory Management
Race Conditions in Order Processing​

Food delivery platforms experience intense race conditions due to concurrent operations from multiple actors (customers ordering, restaurants updating inventory, drivers accepting orders). Top teams test:

Inventory overselling: Multiple customers ordering the last available item simultaneously

Driver double-assignment: Same order assigned to multiple drivers or driver accepting multiple orders that conflict

Order status conflicts: Restaurant marks order ready while customer cancels simultaneously

Stock depletion bugs: Item marked out-of-stock after customer adds to cart but before checkout​

Critical Implementation:

php
// VULNERABLE - Item can be oversold
public function createOrder(Request $request) {
    $item = MenuItem::find($request->item_id);
    if ($item->quantity > 0) {
        $item->decrement('quantity'); // Race condition
        Order::create([...]);
    }
}

// SECURE - Use pessimistic locking
public function createOrder(Request $request) {
    DB::transaction(function() use ($request) {
        $item = MenuItem::where('id', $request->item_id)
            ->lockForUpdate()
            ->first();
        
        if (!$item || $item->quantity < $request->quantity) {
            throw new InsufficientStockException();
        }
        
        if (!$item->is_available || !$item->restaurant->is_open) {
            throw new ItemUnavailableException();
        }
        
        $item->decrement('quantity', $request->quantity);
        
        return Order::create([
            'user_id' => auth()->id(),
            'item_id' => $item->id,
            'quantity' => $request->quantity,
            'price_snapshot' => $item->price, // Store price at order time
            'status' => OrderStatus::PENDING
        ]);
    });
}
Multi-Vendor Order Management​

Order splitting bugs: Cart with items from multiple restaurants must split correctly into separate orders

Partial refund calculations: When one restaurant cancels but others fulfill, refund amount must be accurate

Commission calculation errors: Each vendor's commission calculated independently with proper tax handling

Vendor payout accuracy: Ensure restaurant receives correct amount after platform fees, delivery fees, and discounts

Geolocation & Real-Time Tracking
GPS Tracking Accuracy & Privacy​

Elite teams verify location-based features work reliably:

Delivery radius validation: Restaurants only show to customers within delivery range, accounting for traffic and real-world road networks (not straight-line distance)

ETA calculation accuracy: Estimated delivery time considers real-time traffic, restaurant preparation time, driver availability, and historical data​

GPS drift handling: Driver location updates gracefully handle GPS inaccuracies, tunnels, signal loss

Real-time location privacy: Customer location not exposed to drivers until order accepted, driver location not tracked when off-duty

Geofencing for delivery confirmation: Verify driver is actually at delivery location before allowing "delivered" status​

Race Conditions in Real-Time Updates​

Food delivery apps face unique real-time synchronization challenges:

WebSocket connection timing: Orders created between initial data fetch and WebSocket connection establishment must still appear

Out-of-order message delivery: Status updates arriving out of sequence (e.g., "delivered" arrives before "picked up")

Multi-device synchronization: Order status changes on one device must sync to all user devices

Offline queue handling: Actions taken while offline must resolve conflicts when reconnecting​

Driver Assignment & Route Optimization
Dynamic Driver Assignment Bugs​

Top teams test sophisticated assignment algorithms:

Optimal driver selection: Algorithm considers distance, current load, estimated pickup time, driver ratings, vehicle capacity

Fairness in order distribution: No driver consistently starved or overloaded, equitable distribution of high-value orders

Rebalancing after cancellations: When driver cancels or becomes unavailable, order reassigned without customer impact

Batch order assignment: Multiple orders to same driver optimized for route efficiency​

