Menu Synchronization & Availability
Real-Time Menu Updates​

Menu management across multiple platforms is a critical failure point:

Multi-platform sync failures: Price change in POS must propagate to app, website, and third-party platforms (UberEats, DoorDash) in real-time​

Item availability bugs: Out-of-stock items must be disabled across all channels immediately to prevent orders for unavailable items

Price inconsistency: Same item showing different prices on different platforms

Menu version conflicts: Old cached menu causing orders for discontinued items

Schedule-based availability: Items only available during specific hours (breakfast menu, late-night menu) enforced correctly​

Implementation Requirements:

php
// VULNERABLE - Menu changes not synced
public function updateMenuItem(Request $request, MenuItem $item) {
    $item->update($request->all());
    // Other platforms still show old data
}

// SECURE - Broadcast to all platforms
public function updateMenuItem(Request $request, MenuItem $item) {
    DB::transaction(function() use ($request, $item) {
        $item->update($request->validated());
        
        // Clear all platform caches
        Cache::tags(['menu', "restaurant_{$item->restaurant_id}"])->flush();
        
        // Broadcast real-time update
        broadcast(new MenuItemUpdated($item))->toOthers();
        
        // Sync to third-party platforms
        dispatch(new SyncMenuToThirdPartyPlatforms($item));
        
        // Log for audit trail
        ActivityLog::create([
            'type' => 'menu_update',
            'item_id' => $item->id,
            'changes' => $item->getChanges()
        ]);
    });
}
Order State Machine & Workflow
Order Status Transition Bugs​

Food delivery orders follow complex state machines. Elite teams verify:

Illegal state transitions prevented: Customer can't cancel after driver picked up, restaurant can't mark ready before accepting

Concurrent status updates: Customer and restaurant updating status simultaneously handled correctly

Rollback on failure: If notification fails after status change, transaction rolls back

Timeout handling: Orders stuck in "searching for driver" auto-cancel after threshold

State machine validation: All transitions logged, audit trail maintained​

Critical States to Test:

text
Order Created → Restaurant Accepts → Preparing → Ready → Driver Assigned 
→ Driver Arrived → Picked Up → En Route → Arrived → Delivered

// Test illegal transitions:
- Customer cancels after "Picked Up"
- Restaurant rejects after "Preparing"
- Driver unassigns after "Picked Up"
- Concurrent state changes from multiple actors
Push Notification Delivery
Real-Time Notification Reliability​

Push notifications are mission-critical for order updates:

Token management: Device tokens expire after 270 days, must be refreshed and validated​

Multi-device delivery: Notifications delivered to all user devices (phone, tablet, web)

Priority handling: Critical notifications (order accepted, driver arrived) use high-priority channels, marketing uses normal priority​

Delivery confirmation: Failed deliveries detected and retried, fallback to SMS if push fails

Rate limiting: Bulk notifications staggered to avoid server overload

Timing accuracy: Time-sensitive notifications (driver arriving in 2 minutes) sent immediately​

