2. Real-Time Availability: Menu items marked unavailable must instantly reflect across app, website, third-party platforms.
3. Restaurant Operating Hours: Verify orders only accepted during open hours, accounting for timezone differences.
4. Multi-Restaurant Orders: Cart items from different restaurants must split correctly into separate orders with independent tracking.

=== ORDER STATE MACHINE ===
1. Illegal Transitions: Prevent invalid status changes:
- No cancellation after "picked_up" status
- No restaurant rejection after "preparing"
- No driver unassignment after "en_route"
2. Concurrent Updates: Use optimistic locking or database transactions when multiple actors update order status.
3. Timeout Handling: Orders in "searching_for_driver" > 15 min auto-cancel with customer notification.
4. Rollback on Failure: If notification/webhook fails after status change, entire transaction rolls back.

=== DRIVER ASSIGNMENT & ROUTING ===
1. Assignment Algorithm: Verify driver selection considers:
- Distance to restaurant + restaurant to customer
- Current driver load and route optimization for multi-order deliveries
- Driver ratings and acceptance rate
- Vehicle capacity for large orders
2. Double Assignment Prevention: Use database locks to prevent same order assigned to multiple drivers.
3. Reassignment Logic: When driver cancels, order immediately reassigned without customer manual intervention.
4. ETA Accuracy: Delivery time = restaurant_prep_time + driver_travel_time + traffic_factor. Update dynamically.

=== GEOLOCATION & TRACKING ===
1. Delivery Radius: Calculate using road network distance (Google Maps Distance Matrix), not straight-line.
2. GPS Accuracy: Handle GPS drift, signal loss gracefully. Don't mark "delivered" if driver >50m from address.
3. Real-Time Updates: WebSocket connection for live driver tracking. Handle reconnection, out-of-order messages.
4. Privacy: Customer full address hidden from driver until order accepted. Driver not tracked when offline.

=== MENU SYNCHRONIZATION ===
1. Multi-Platform Sync: Menu changes must propagate to:
- Mobile app (clear cache, push update)
- Website (invalidate CDN cache)
- Third-party platforms (API webhook to UberEats/DoorDash)
2. Price Consistency: Same item same price everywhere. Cache invalidation on price update.
3. Availability Sync: Out-of-stock items disabled everywhere within 30 seconds.
4. Schedule-Based Menus: Breakfast/lunch/dinner menus show only during configured hours.

=== PUSH NOTIFICATIONS ===
1. Token Management: Expire tokens >270 days old. De-duplicate multiple tokens per user.
2. Delivery Confirmation: Track failed deliveries, retry 3x, fallback to SMS if all attempts fail.
3. Priority Handling: Use FCM high-priority for order status, normal for marketing.
4. Multi-Device: Send to all user devices. Sync read status across devices.
5. Rate Limiting: Stagger bulk sends to avoid server overload (max 1000/minute).

=== SECURITY VULNERABILITIES ===
1. SQL Injection: All queries use parameter binding or Eloquent ORM. Check raw queries: DB::select('... WHERE id = ?', [$id])
2. Mass Assignment: All models define $fillable. Verify no sensitive fields (is_admin, commission_rate) mass-assignable.
3. Authorization: Check Gates/Policies:
- Customers can only view/cancel own orders
- Restaurants can only manage own menu/orders
- Drivers can only update assigned orders
4. File Uploads: Restaurant logo/menu images validated for MIME type, size, stored outside public directory.
5. Rate Limiting: API endpoints throttled (60 req/min general, 5 req/min login).

=== RACE CONDITIONS ===
1. Order Creation: Lock menu item inventory during order placement.
2. Driver Assignment: Lock order record during driver assignment algorithm.
3. Payment Processing: Idempotency keys prevent duplicate charges.
4. Coupon Usage: Lock coupon record during redemption check.
5. Refund Processing: Lock order financial records during refund calculation.

=== TESTING REQUIREMENTS ===
1. Feature Tests: Test complete order workflows end-to-end:
- Customer creates order → Restaurant accepts → Driver assigned → Delivered
- Customer cancels before restaurant accepts
- Restaurant rejects order → Customer refunded
2. Race Condition Tests: Use parallel HTTP requests to test concurrent actions:
// Test 10 users ordering last item simultaneously
Promise::all([
Http::async()->post('/orders', ['item_id' => $lastItem]),
// ... repeat 10 times
])->wait();
// Verify only 1 order succeeded, 9 got "out of stock"

